# Western digits and EGP in UI

User-visible **numbers the app generates** must use ASCII digits `0`–`9`, even when `Locale` is Arabic. Arabic **words** (labels, month names, etc.) and RTL layout stay localized; the app locale is not forced to English.

**Money suffix:** always uppercase `EGP` in Arabic and English UI (`MoneyFormat.currencyLabel`). Do not show `ج.م` on app-formatted amounts.

## Central API

`lib/core/formatting/display_digits.dart`:

- `DisplayDigits.decimalPattern()` / `formatInteger` — grouping with `en_US` digit shapes only
- `DisplayDigits.toWesternDigits` — normalize Eastern Arabic numerals in **input** before parse

**Money:** `MoneyFormat.formatPiastres` / `withoutCurrencySuffix` (via `AppAmount`). **Percents:** `BasisPoints.formatPercent` / `parsePercentInput`.

Do not use `NumberFormat.decimalPattern('ar')` for on-screen amounts. Dates: when formatting for display, force Western digits in the numeric parts (see `DateFormat` call sites).

**Layout:** Western/LTR runs use `LtrIsolate`; alignment follows locale start/end — see `bnood-bidi-layout.mdc` (do not force `TextDirection.ltr` on full lines in RTL headers).

Stored DB values and user free text are unchanged; parsing accepts Eastern digits where we already parse amounts.

## Tests

AR locale widget tests must assert `0`–`9` and `EGP` in money output, not `٠`–`٩` or `ج.م`.
